> Couple of tips:

...and some annotations.

> 1) if you are a spellcaster (wizard, healer etc.) try to steer away
> from metal armour, especially metal body armor. Use leather or DSM

You won't see any DSM - a dragon scale male - early, so you can forget
about it for a while. :-)

> instead (realistically leather early game, unless you get a wish early
> on)

Using wishes will require to know in detail what's existing and what's
best to wish for; this requires to allow oneself being spoiled - like
getting the knowledge about the existance of DSM and how to exactly wish
for one (spelling!).

> 2) as a monk, avoid body armor in the early game ENITRELY or you'll
> hit nothing
> 3) If you plan to melee stuff, then try to stay unburdened. That extra
> hit the monster gets could be the difference between life and death.

Stay unburdened even if you don't melee!

> 4)Don't just drink from fountains. They can release some nasty
> monsters

To a newbie I'd suggest to ignore fountains completely, especially in
shallow levels where the character is likely not yet well developed.

> 5) if you see an "n", and it is staying still, then move AROUND it. Do
> NOT melee it. It can and will steal your items.
> 6)Don't melee little blue "e"s until you have a source of reflection!

Having a mirror just in your inventory won't make it safe to kill a
floating eye. Use ranged attacks; throw darts, stones, daggers at it.

> Whilst they cannot harm you, they can freeze you, and other stuff can
> come and kick your ass!
> 7)Try not to attack shopkeepers :)

Just don't attack shopkeepers, and avoid attacking peacefuls and tame
monsters.

